<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dilmun Burial Mounds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2050–1750 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> Burial Mounds are the tens of thousands of earth and stone tumuli built across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> mainly between about 2050 and 1750 BCE, in the Early Dilmun period. They range from small individual graves to large royal tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aali">A'ali</a>. Many have been lost to modern building, and twenty-one archaeological sites were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dilmun-burial-mounds.jpg" alt="Dilmun Burial Mounds" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Storrs-Fox, Edwin Aubrey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burial_Mounds_in_Bahrain_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
